### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to filter by a computed date boundary using `LocalDate` arithmetic and a stream predicate. This tests date API knowledge, boundary precision (inclusive vs exclusive), and testability with `Clock`.

### How do you find employees who joined in the last 5 years?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class RecentJoiners {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        LocalDate cutoff = LocalDate.now().minusYears(5);

        List<Employee> recentJoiners = employees.stream()
            .filter(e -> !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(cutoff))
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Cutoff: " + cutoff);
        recentJoiners.forEach(e ->
            System.out.println(e.getName() + " joined " + e.getJoinDate()));
    }
}
```

```text
Cutoff: 2020-01-15
Priya joined 2021-06-01
Neha joined 2022-03-15
Ananya joined 2023-01-10
Meera joined 2024-07-20
```

The `!isBefore` predicate means "on or after": it includes employees who joined on the exact cutoff day.

### Why use !isBefore instead of isAfter?

| Expression | Meaning | Includes cutoff day? |
|---|---|---|
| !joinDate.isBefore(cutoff) | On or after cutoff | Yes (inclusive) |
| joinDate.isAfter(cutoff) | Strictly after cutoff | No (exclusive) |
| joinDate.isEqual(cutoff) \|\| joinDate.isAfter(cutoff) | On or after | Yes (verbose) |

```java
LocalDate cutoff = LocalDate.of(2020, 1, 15);
LocalDate exact = LocalDate.of(2020, 1, 15);

System.out.println(!exact.isBefore(cutoff));  // true (included)
System.out.println(exact.isAfter(cutoff));    // false (excluded!)
```

Using `isAfter` alone creates an off-by-one bug for employees who joined on the exact cutoff day.

### How do you make it testable with Clock?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.time.*;
import java.util.*;

public class RecentJoinerService {
    private final Clock clock;

    public RecentJoinerService(Clock clock) {
        this.clock = clock;
    }

    public List<Employee> findRecentJoiners(
            List<Employee> employees, int years) {
        LocalDate cutoff = LocalDate.now(clock).minusYears(years);
        return employees.stream()
            .filter(e -> !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(cutoff))
            .toList();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Production: system clock
        var service = new RecentJoinerService(Clock.systemDefaultZone());

        // Test: fixed clock for deterministic results
        Clock fixedClock = Clock.fixed(
            LocalDate.of(2025, 1, 15).atStartOfDay(ZoneId.systemDefault()).toInstant(),
            ZoneId.systemDefault());
        var testService = new RecentJoinerService(fixedClock);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: computing the cutoff inside the predicate.

```java
// WRONG: creates a new LocalDate for every employee
List<Employee> wrong = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(
        LocalDate.now().minusYears(5)))  // computed N times!
    .toList();

// CORRECT: compute once outside
LocalDate cutoff = LocalDate.now().minusYears(5);
List<Employee> correct = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(cutoff))
    .toList();
```

Mistake 2: not handling null joining dates.

```java
// WRONG: NullPointerException if joinDate is null
List<Employee> wrong = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(cutoff))
    .toList();

// CORRECT: null guard
List<Employee> correct = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getJoinDate() != null
        && !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(cutoff))
    .toList();
```

### How do you group recent joiners by year?

```java
Map<Integer, List<Employee>> byYear = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(cutoff))
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        e -> e.getJoinDate().getYear()));

byYear.forEach((year, emps) ->
    System.out.println(year + ": " + emps.size() + " joiners"));
```

```text
2021: 1 joiners
2022: 1 joiners
2023: 1 joiners
2024: 1 joiners
```

### What about Period-based calculations?

```java
import java.time.Period;

// Calculate tenure for each recent joiner
employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !e.getJoinDate().isBefore(cutoff))
    .forEach(e -> {
        Period tenure = Period.between(e.getJoinDate(), LocalDate.now());
        System.out.println(e.getName() + ": "
            + tenure.getYears() + "y " + tenure.getMonths() + "m");
    });
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find employees who joined in a specific month?" Filter with `getJoinDate().getMonth() == Month.JANUARY`.
- "How do you find employees who joined on a weekend?" Filter with `getJoinDate().getDayOfWeek().getValue() >= 6`.
- "How do you parameterize the number of years?" Accept it as a method argument and pass to `minusYears(n)`.
